        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lamont%2C_Oklahoma"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town of Lamont was named for Secretary of War Daniel Scott Lamont.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.okhistory.org/publications/enc/entry?entry=LA013"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Lamont was founded by the Lamont Townsite Company after the area opened to non-Indian settlers in 1893.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6,
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.okhistory.org/publications/enc/entry?entry=LA013"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Blackwell, Enid and Southwestern Railroad reached Lamont on November 15, 1900, helping it develop as an agricultural community.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8,
        9,
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.okhistory.org/publications/enc/entry?entry=LA013"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Dayton School in Lamont is listed in the National Register of Historic Places.",
